course: dictionary
lesson: 8253
title: One word, many meanings: rib
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The clues around the word show you which meaning the writer wants. «Twelve pairs of ribs curve round from the spine like a cage» is about bones in the body, because of the words spine, cage and heart in the sentence.
explain: Always check by swapping in your meaning. In «The Amazon giant water lily has leaves held up by stiff ribs underneath», ribs are not bones at all. They are stiff strips that support the leaf, like the ribs of an umbrella. Does your meaning still make sense in the sentence?
explain: Rib can also be a verb. If someone says «Dad likes to rib me about my haircut», they are teasing me in a friendly way. This is informal, so you will hear it in chatty speech more than in formal writing. Look at the words around the word to decide what kind of rib it is.
fact: A rib is one of the curved bones around your chest.
fact: Humans have 12 pairs of ribs, which makes 24 ribs in all.
fact: Your rib cage protects your heart and lungs, and it moves when you breathe.
fact: «Ribs» can also mean meat with a rib bone in it, such as spare ribs.
fact: As a verb, «rib» (informal) means to tease someone in a friendly way.
exercise: «Twelve pairs of ribs curve round from the spine like a cage.» What are ribs in this sentence? | *Curved bones around the chest | Teasing jokes | Strips of meat on a plate | Leaves of a water lily
exercise: «The Amazon giant water lily has leaves held up by stiff ribs underneath.» What do the ribs do here? | Make the leaf taste sweet | Hide the leaf from the sun | *Support the leaf, like the ribs of an umbrella | Protect the heart and lungs
exercise: Dad says he will rib you about your haircut. What does rib mean? | To break a bone | *To tease someone in a friendly way, but in a chatty, informal way | To tease someone in a friendly way | To cook meat slowly
test: «A cobra spreads long ribs in its neck to stretch the skin into a hood.» What does the hood tell the reader? | The cobra is lost | The cobra is ready to sleep | The cobra is cold and wants a blanket | *I am big, so leave me alone
test: «A tortoise's shell is part of its skeleton, joined to its ribs and backbone, so it can never leave it.» Why can a tortoise never leave its shell? | Tortoises have no bones at all | *The shell is part of its skeleton, joined to its ribs and backbone | The shell is a coat it can take off | It is glued on with mud
exam: Sam cracked a rib when he fell off his bike. What is a rib in this sentence? | A friendly tease | A strip of leaf | A type of bicycle part you can buy | *One of the curved bones around your chest
